//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): `drain` a mailbox that really is empty —
// which mints `<drained!>` — and then call `abandon` instead of `release`.
//
// `abandon` accepts only `<!open>`; `drained` is a different state. This
// pins the other half of the ratchet from `negative_release_without_drain`:
// there it was "you may not skip the check", here it is "once you HAVE
// checked and it succeeded, the only next move is `release`" — a caller
// cannot launder a legitimately drained mailbox through the leak-a-ring
// escape hatch just because both tors happen to take a `*Mailbox` and
// return nothing.
//
// The C has no opinion here either: `uk_mbox_free` would run exactly the
// same free either way once the ring is actually empty, so nothing is lost
// by calling it through `release`. This is the compiler refusing a
// meaningless detour, not a safety hole in the C.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch: expected
//           'unikraft.mpi:open' but got 'unikraft.mpi:drained!' for
//           argument 'mbox'

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file
// would otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/mpi
~import std/io

~unikraft/mpi:create(capacity: 4)
| ok m0 |> unikraft/mpi:drain(mbox: m0)
    | ok drained |> unikraft/mpi:abandon(mbox: drained)
    | pending stuck |> std/io:print.ln("should never get here: {{ stuck.count:d }} pending on a fresh mailbox")
| failed why |> std/io:print.ln("create failed: {{ why:s }}")
